(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).lsat=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat.full||[]).push({
  num: 8, part: `Part Two: The Witnesses`, title: `Counting Jackets`, weeks: `May 2027`,
  tasks: [`LR05`,`LR06`,`LR07`,`LR08`,`LR09`,`LR10`,`LR17`,`LR18`,`RC01`,`RC03`,`RC04`,`RC05`,`RC06`,`RC13`],
  cast: [
    [`Luis Ruiz`,`Dani's father, at Sterling. Turns sixty two on Thursday, May 6. Calls Wednesdays at seven, most weeks.`],
    [`Ana Brennan`,`Appellate lawyer on Federal Boulevard. Asked the court in March to let a lab look under Travis Hensley's nails.`],
    [`Gene Vigil`,`Retired Commerce City detective. Built the case in 2007, and gave Dani his notebook in January.`],
    [`Meredith Sloane`,`Chief deputy district attorney in Brighton. Answers the motion this month, on paper, and doesn't waste a word.`],
    [`Teo Ruiz`,`Dani's brother, 30. Hasn't answered her since April.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `Wednesday, May 5, at seven, she sat on her step with the phone in her lap and the basement light on, and the phone didn't ring. At ten after she checked that it was charged. At half past she went up to the kitchen and stood there with the phone in her hand. In all the years of Wednesdays he'd missed four, and every one of those had been a lockdown she only heard about from him, a week late.`,
    `Thursday, May 6, at seven, it rang. The recording, and then him. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"It's Thursday,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"I know what day it is, Pa."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Sixty two. I traded Oscar my Wednesday for his Thursday. I wanted to hear you on the day."</span>`,
    `Her card had come. He read her the inside of it like she hadn't written it, slow, with his reading glasses on, she could tell. Somebody on the tier had made a cake out of crushed cookies and peanut butter in a commissary bag and stuck a match in it. Rudy sang. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Rudy can't sing,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Nobody told him in time."</span>`,
    `She asked about the board.`,
    `Since Sterling it had sat on the kitchen table exactly the way she'd left it, her pawn on the square where his bishop used to be, his queen at the far end of the file that hadn't opened. Both Wednesdays since, he'd said the same thing. He said it again. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I'm still looking at it."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Dani">"I told Teo what you said. About the pawn."</span> A long breath on the line. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"What'd he say?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Nothing."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"That's him saying something,"</span> her father said.`,
    `Meredith Sloane's answer to the motion had come in on Monday. She'd asked the court for two more weeks and gotten them and used every day. Ana had read it on her phone at a red light, she said, and sat through the green. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Tuesday,"</span> she'd said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Bring a calculator. Meredith brought numbers."</span>`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ana's Pencil: counting`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Count and share`,
        body: [`May on Federal. The laundromat has its door propped open for the first time this year, the draft comes up the stairs smelling like dryer sheets, and Dicta has moved her whole self into it. Pencil.`,
          `This test has no math on it. It has numbers anyway, in a few questions on every test, and nearly all of them break the same way. Somebody moves from a share to a count, or from a count to a share, and hopes you don't notice the move.`,
          `A share is a part of something. Nine percent of the accidents. One in five people in the room. A count is how many. Twenty one men. Four burglaries. They answer different questions, and an argument that needs one of them can't run on the other. Complaints about the bus line were a smaller share of the city's complaints this year? Fine. If the city got twice as many complaints, a smaller share can still be more complaints about the bus. Burglaries on your street doubled? Doubled from two is four.`,
          `Averages hide things too. An average is a total divided by a count, and one huge number in the total can drag it anywhere. One neighbor's lottery win can raise the average income on a street where nobody else got a raise. When an argument moves from an average to what's ordinary or typical, ask what went into the total.`,
          `So when you see a percent, ask of what, and how many. When you see a raw number, ask out of how many. When you see an average, ask what's in it. The trap answers will be very confident about whichever one the argument handed you.`],
        terms: [[`Share`,`A part compared to a whole, as a percent or a fraction. It says nothing about how many unless you know the whole.`],[`Count`,`How many. It says nothing about how big a part it is unless you know the whole.`]],
        exam: `Watch for flaw and weaken questions built on percentages, rates or totals. The right answer usually names the missing number: the total, the size of the group, or what the percent was taken of.` },
      { h: `Who got counted`,
        body: [`Then there's where the numbers came from. A sample is a small group standing in for a big one, and it's only as good as the way it got picked. People who fill out a comment card. People who answer a poll on a website. The fights somebody phoned in to the police. Each of those groups picked itself, and a group that picks itself tends to share whatever you're trying to measure.`,
          `Size matters less than people think. Four hundred people chosen at random can tell you a lot. Four hundred people who chose to answer can tell you about people who choose to answer. When an answer says the sample was too small, check whether it's the size that's wrong or the picking. On this test it's usually the picking.`,
          `Police files are samples too, by the way, and nobody ever calls them that. A detective's experience is the cases somebody reported to him. The fights that ended quietly out back, with nobody watching, never made it into his notebook, so they never made it into his rule.`],
        terms: [[`Representative sample`,`A sample that looks like the whole group in the ways that matter to the question.`],[`Self-selected sample`,`A sample whose members chose to be in it, so they may differ from everyone else.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask what the argument overlooks, or takes for granted, about its survey or study. Ask how the people in it got there before you ask how many there were.` },
      { h: `Out of how many`,
        body: [`Most kitchen fires start while somebody's home. My sister read that in a magazine once and wanted to cook with the front door open, I never found out why. Nearly all cooking happens while somebody's home. A number about when most of the fires start tells you nothing until you set it next to when most of the cooking happens. That comparison number is the base rate: how common a thing is in the whole population before you know anything else.`,
          `It cuts the other way as well. A base rate tells you what a trait alone is worth. Twenty red jackets in a room, and the jacket alone points at any one of the twenty. But a base rate treats every one of the twenty as the same, and they rarely are. Add one fact that sets a person apart and the base rate stops being the whole story. An argument that leans on a base rate gets weaker with anything that makes the group's members unequal.`],
        terms: [[`Base rate`,`How common something is in a whole population, before any other information is considered.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask which fact most weakens an argument from frequencies, or what it fails to consider. The answer is often the comparison number nobody gave you, or a fact that makes one member of the group different from the rest.` },
      { h: `Questions that cut both ways`,
        body: [`An evaluate question asks what you'd most want to know to judge an argument. The answers are questions, or whether clauses, and the right one has an odd property. Answer it yes and the argument gets stronger. Answer it no and it gets weaker. Either way it moves.`,
          `So test each choice twice. Imagine the answer is yes. Then imagine no. If one of them leaves the argument exactly where it was, that choice is gone. The usual wrong answer helps a lot if it comes back one way and does nothing the other way. The next most usual is a question that's interesting and has nothing to do with the conclusion.`,
          `Try it on a small one. A woman says her tomatoes did better this year because she switched fertilizers. Would you want to know whether her neighbor's tomatoes, on the old fertilizer, also did better this year? Yes. If they did, it was probably the weather. If they didn't, the fertilizer looks good. It moves both ways, so it's the one.`],
        terms: [[`Evaluate`,`A question whose answer, either way, would change how strong the argument is.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask which would be most useful to know, or which question's answer would most help in evaluating the argument. Run every choice as a yes and then as a no.` },
      { h: `What the record says`,
        body: [`Records are the dullest papers in a case file and some of the most useful. A property log, a lab sheet, a dispatch line. When a passage is a record, read it three ways. What does it state? Dates, places, what was done, in its own words. What does it let you infer? The thing nobody wrote down but somebody must have done or believed. And whose voice is in it? A clerk who types up what a detective said hasn't said they agree.`,
          `That last one matters on attitude questions. A dry document usually has a dry author, and neutral can be the right answer when a passage only reports. It's rarer on full passages, where most authors lean one way, and indifferent is almost never right. But look for the one line where somebody's reasoning got written down, and keep it attached to the person it came from.`,
          `And read the dates. A clerk writes received, stored, returned and audited in that order because that's the order things happened to the bag. Nobody wrote it to win anything, which is why it can be worth more than most of the arguments in the file.`],
        terms: [[`Inference`,`Something the passage doesn't say but that must be so, or very likely is, given what it does say.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask what the passage states, what can be inferred from it, the author's attitude, or which fact would most weaken a claim in it. Keep a quoted view with the person quoted. A record usually keeps time better than the people in it did, so check the dates before you trust anybody's memory of them.` }
    ],
    video: [`Ry1g5A3N1Qw`,`How to Master Number and Percentage Questions in LSAT Logical Reasoning`,`LSAT Unplugged & Law School Admissions Podcast`,`8:06`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f8s1`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR06`, tags:[`STAT`], title:`One in five`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, May 11. Ana had the response printed on white paper with her pencil already all over page two, and a calculator out of a drawer that had to be older than Dani, with a solar strip that only worked if you held it under the lamp.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="People's response to the defendant's motion, filed Monday, May 3, 2027, page two">The defense makes much of the red jacket's being common. It wasn't. Mr. Galvan testified at trial that about one hundred twenty people were in the Night Owl that night. The crew list in Detective Vigil's notes, taken from the shift supervisor, has twenty one names, the defendant's among them. Even if every man on it was in the bar, fewer than one in five people in that room wore the jacket. And of all of them, exactly one man was seen fighting Travis Hensley.</prop>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Twenty one over a hundred twenty,"</span> Ana said, and punched it in under the lamp, and turned the calculator around. 17.5. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Her arithmetic's fine. It usually is. So what's wrong with the argument?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Seventeen and a half percent isn't rare. Nearly one person in five is a lot of any room."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Ana shakes her head and says Dani's fighting on Meredith's ground. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Make the room five hundred people and she says four percent, and then where are you? Don't argue the share. Count."</span>`,
          why:`Flaw. Right sentence, wrong fight. You stayed inside her percent, which is where she wanted you. How big the share is was never the trouble. A share can't do the job her argument needs done until you turn it back into a count.`},
        {t:`"Ronnie's hundred and twenty was a guess. He was pouring by himself all night. He never once counted a soul in there."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana asks how many jackets there'd be if the room held a thousand people. Twenty one. Sixty people? Twenty one, still. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Then the size of the room isn't where she's wrong,"</span> Ana says, and writes 120? in the margin anyway, small, for later.`,
          why:`Flaw. You went after the bottom of the fraction, and the mistake's not down there. Ronnie could be off by fifty people either way and the jacket count sits right where it was, at twenty one.`},
        {t:`"A share isn't a count. On her own numbers, under one in five of that room is twenty one jackets. She needed one."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Say it to the judge just like that."</span> In the margin she writes 21, and next to it 1, and a line between them. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"You can't identify a man with a jacket twenty one men own. She picked the number that makes twenty men sound like nobody."</span>`,
          why:`Flaw. The response argues from a share to a conclusion that needs a count. Twenty one men owned that jacket. Put them in a room of a hundred twenty or a room of a thousand, it's still twenty one, and a jacket only identifies somebody if he's the one man wearing it.`},
        {t:`"Then more than eighty percent didn't have one on. So the man in red was nearly sure to be one of the crew."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana asks who ever said the man at the curb came out of the bar. Nobody had. Dani had done Meredith's homework for her, and thrown in a little extra for free.`,
          why:`Flaw. It turns the share into a new claim the numbers don't reach. Most of the room had no jacket, fine. That tells you about the room. It doesn't tell you the man at the curb was ever in it, and nobody has shown he was.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s2`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR08`, tags:[`STAT`], title:`Seen`, q:`Which reply most weakens Meredith's sentence about the fight?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana underlined the last sentence twice. The second line came out wobbly, because Dicta picked that moment to lean her whole weight on Ana's shin. Exactly one man was seen fighting Travis Hensley.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's the one she believes,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The rest of the paragraph is upholstery. She'll say it at the hearing in just those words, and she'll say it like it's arithmetic too."</span> She set the pencil down on the page and pushed Dicta off her shin. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Weaken it."</span>`,
        `Dani knew the patrol report by heart now, the way she knew the bus schedule. Patrons' names not obtained. And Ronnie in January, across the table at the taqueria with a towel on his shoulder, saying it like it was nothing: I couldn't see over the people.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Ronnie never saw what started it, by his own account. He only looked up when the yelling got loud."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Which makes him a worse witness to the start,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"He still saw two men fighting. She didn't say anything about how it started. She said who was seen."</span>`,
          why:`Weaken. This goes after Ronnie on how the fight began, and Meredith never claimed he saw the beginning. She claimed one man was seen fighting. Ronnie saw that much, whatever he missed before it.`},
        {t:`"Seen by Ronnie, who couldn't see over the crowd. Nobody else in that room was ever asked."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Ana says seen is a word about who was looking. Then she counts it off on the desk with the pencil: one bartender, behind the bar, a Series night, and an officer who never wrote down a single customer's name. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It's true, what she wrote,"</span> she says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Because they only ever asked the one man."</span>`,
          why:`Weaken. Meredith's sentence rides on the word seen. Seen by whom? By Ronnie, who couldn't see over the crowd, and he's the only one anybody ever asked. One witness asked gets you one fight seen, whatever else went on in that room.`},
        {t:`"Pa told Gene that Sunday he never touched anybody. So nobody saw him fight at all."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana looks at her over the page and doesn't say anything, and doesn't have to. He'd told her at a table in April that he'd hit him first.`,
          why:`Weaken. A denial the defendant has since taken back can't weaken anything. It also confuses what Luis said with what someone saw.`},
        {t:`"Ronnie only knew him by the jacket. Any man on the crew looked like Pa from behind a bar."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"He knew him by name,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Saturdays, for years, report cards in his wallet. Don't spend Ronnie on a thing he never got wrong."</span>`,
          why:`Weaken. Contradicted. Ronnie knew Luis as a regular and named him the night of the fight, so the jacket wasn't how he knew him.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s3`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC05`, title:`Item 7`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, May 13. Meredith had attached four exhibits. Ana had read all four and put three back in the folder. The fourth she'd copied onto yellow paper, which meant pencil.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Commerce City Police Department, Property and Evidence, item record, People's Exhibit D">ITEM 7. Fingernail scrapings and clippings, victim T. Hensley, right and left hands. Two sealed envelopes in one evidence bag. Collected at autopsy 10/28/07. Received 10/29/07, 0810 hrs, from Det. G. Vigil. Stored frozen, evidence room.<br>10/30/07. Submitted to the state crime lab with Item 3 (red jacket, right cuff) and Item 4 (victim's shirt) for DNA analysis.<br>11/06/07. Lab request for Item 7 cancelled by Det. G. Vigil. Reason given: victim and suspect in a physical fight inside the bar before the assault; any foreign material under the victim's nails is expected from that fight and of no probative value as to who struck the victim in the lot. Items 3 and 4 to proceed.<br>11/08/07. Item 7 returned from the lab unopened. Seals intact. Returned to frozen storage.<br>Audits 2012, 2017, 2022. Item present. Seals intact.<br>04/14/27. Location confirmed at request of the District Attorney's Office. Seals intact. No analysis has been performed on this item.</prop>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Read it like a lease,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The easy part first. Where has it been?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`At the state lab since 2007, where it sat untested until the jacket results came back.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Eleven eight oh seven,"</span> Ana says, and taps it. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Returned. Two days after Gene called. It's been in their freezer ever since."</span>`,
          why:`Specific detail. Contradicted. The record says the lab sent the item back unopened on November 8, 2007, and it went back into frozen storage.`},
        {t:`In the evidence freezer since 2007, to the lab and back unopened, its seals checked at every audit.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Every audit,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Somebody opened that freezer every five years, looked at the bag, wrote seals intact and shut the door. Nobody ever lost it. Nobody ever looked inside it either."</span>`,
          why:`Specific detail. The record puts it in frozen storage from October 29, 2007, at the lab and back unopened in November, and present with seals intact at the 2012, 2017 and 2022 audits and again in April.`},
        {t:`In the evidence room until April, when the district attorney's office had it moved for testing.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Confirmed,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Not moved. Meredith had somebody go look at it. She wanted to know it was there before she said yes."</span>`,
          why:`Specific detail. The April entry says the location was confirmed. Nothing says the item moved.`},
        {t:`In a freezer from the day of the autopsy on, unopened and untouched by anybody since then.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Close,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Collected the twenty eighth, received the twenty ninth. And it went across town to a lab and came back. Untouched is a word the record never uses."</span>`,
          why:`Specific detail. Unopened is right. But the record has it received the day after the autopsy and sent to the lab and back, so from the day of the autopsy and untouched both go past it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s4`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC03`, title:`Whose voice`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Now who's talking,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Somebody in a property room typed this, a line at a time, over twenty years. Whoever typed the November entries. What does that person think about those scrapings?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Sure they'd never matter. It calls them of no probative value, in so many words.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Who calls them that?"</span> Ana says. Reason given, then the reason. The words belong to Gene. The clerk only wrote them down.`,
          why:`Author's attitude. The words are in the record, but they come after "reason given," so they're the detective's. The author is reporting a view, not holding one.`},
        {t:`Uneasy. It notes the seals over and over again, as if somebody knew it should have been tested.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's every bag in the room,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Seals intact is what you write on an audit. You're reading a feeling into a checklist."</span>`,
          why:`Author's attitude. Unsupported. The seal entries are routine audit notes, and nothing on the page shows unease.`},
        {t:`Nothing, as far as the page shows. It writes down Gene's reason and his name, and stops there.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Nothing,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Which is the right answer more often than you'd like. Whoever typed it put his name on it. They didn't put their own on his reason."</span>`,
          why:`Author's attitude. Neutral. The record states what was done and attributes the reason to Detective Vigil. Its author never judges whether he was right.`},
        {t:`Annoyed with the lab, for sending the bag back unopened when it could have run it anyway.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The lab did what it was told,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Gene cancelled it. Nobody in this record is annoyed at anybody. That's what makes it so awful to read."</span>`,
          why:`Author's attitude. Unsupported. The record says the request was cancelled and the item came back, with no comment on the lab.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s5`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC06`, title:`Expected`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Now the part nobody typed,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It went to the lab in the same box as the jacket. A week later Gene cancelled it, and two days after that it came back unopened, and the jacket stayed. Why wasn't it tested? Don't tell me what you think. Tell me what this page lets you say."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Gene figured the fight explained whatever was under there, and the jacket was going ahead.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Expected,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"He doesn't say I had my guy. He says expected. That's how you say I had my guy in a property room."</span>`,
          why:`Inference. The reason given is that anything under the nails would be expected from the fight, and the same entry sends the jacket and shirt ahead. So the record supports that he thought the test couldn't tell him anything new, with the jacket already on its way.`},
        {t:`Gene thought the scrapings would only show Travis's own skin, so there'd be nothing to look at.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Nothing useful, yes,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"But he says why. Somebody else's skin, from the fight. Not Travis's own."</span>`,
          why:`Inference. Half right. He did think nothing useful would come of it. But the record says he expected material from the fight, which means another man's, not the victim's.`},
        {t:`The lab ran short of money for DNA work that fall and sent back whatever wasn't urgent.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Find the lab's money on that page,"</span> Ana says. It isn't there. The lab did two items and returned the third because a detective called.`,
          why:`Inference. Unsupported. The record names who cancelled the request and why, and it isn't the lab or its budget.`},
        {t:`Phil asked the State not to test them, in case they came back with Pa's DNA under the nails.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Phil never got a say in what the police sent to a lab,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"And the line has one name on it."</span>`,
          why:`Inference. Contradicted. The cancellation is recorded as Detective Vigil's, in November 2007, for his own stated reason.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s6`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC13`, title:`Under there`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana read Gene's line out loud, the way she read things she meant to take apart. Any foreign material under the victim's nails is expected from that fight and of no probative value as to who struck the victim in the lot.`,
        `Dani didn't hear the end of it. She was still on expected.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Dani">"He's right about part of it,"</span> she said. Ana looked up. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"His will be under there. I saw his neck that Sunday."</span>`,
        `Then she told it, the way it had come back to her in January at the bathroom sink. Noon on a Sunday, the bathroom door open, her father shaving, who never shaved on Sundays. A square of tissue under his jaw on the left side with a red dot coming through it. Cut myself, mija. Go back to sleep. A nick under his collar by the time Gene came at three. She said it to the edge of the desk. She'd never once said it out loud, and it came out shorter than it had been in her head for four months. The last time she'd told anybody what she saw in that house on that Sunday, she was fifteen, and there were twelve of them listening, and she'd been sure.`,
        `Ana didn't write anything down. She laid the pencil crosswise on the yellow page and kept her hand on it. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Since January,"</span> she said. Not a question. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"I didn't know what it was."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It's what Ronnie said it was. Two men with their hands on each other in a bar."</span> She picked the pencil back up. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Good. Now it's mine too. I'd a lot rather have it from you than from a lab."</span>`,
        `She tapped Gene's line. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"So say his is there. What's wrong with this sentence?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`It assumes the fight inside is the only way anybody's skin got under there. Tests can often tell two men apart, and the man at the curb got close enough to hit him.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Ana writes TWO MEN on the yellow page and boxes it. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Expected tells you what'll be there,"</span> she says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It doesn't tell you what else will. If there's one and it's his, Meredith wrote the right brief. If there's two."</span> She doesn't finish it. Dani doesn't ask her to.`,
          why:`Strengthen or weaken a passage claim. Gene's line says the nails can't tell you anything about the lot, since Luis's DNA ought to be there from the bar. That holds only if he's the only man whose DNA could be there. If a test can separate two men, and the man at the curb got close enough to strike Travis, a second profile would be about the lot.`},
        {t:`The tests are a lot better now than they were back in 2007. A lab today could find things under those nails that Gene never once pictured, sitting in his kitchen.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Better at what, though?"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Gene's line isn't about what the lab could do. It's about whose skin he thought it'd find. Go after that."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen or weaken a passage claim. Better tests might help, but Gene's reasoning isn't about technique. It assumes only one man's DNA could be there, and this choice never touches that.`},
        {t:`Pa told Gene that Sunday he never fought anybody that night, so Gene had no reason in the world to expect his skin under there, from the bar or anywhere else.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Gene had Ronnie,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"And you just told me about the neck. Don't argue the thing you know isn't so."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen or weaken a passage claim. Gene's reason rested on Ronnie's account of the fight, which Luis has since admitted. Luis's denial doesn't weaken the line.`},
        {t:`Gene cancelled the test before the jacket ever came back from the state lab, so he couldn't have known anything yet about whose blood was on that cuff.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Couldn't have known what?"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The jacket doesn't tell you what's under a man's nails. That's timing. The line's wrong for a different reason."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen or weaken a passage claim. When he cancelled it doesn't bear on whether foreign DNA would be of no value about the lot.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s7`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR06`, tags:[`STAT`], title:`Page eleven`, q:`Gene's reasoning from the ten or eleven calls is most vulnerable to which criticism?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday, May 15, nine in the morning, Gene's notebook out of its freezer bag on Ana's desk. Ana had flagged page eleven with a strip of yellow paper. A column of names in Gene's square hand, two of them misspelled and fixed, and across the top, PVR DAY SHIFT 10/27 PER SUPV. Twenty one names. The fourth one down was RUIZ, L.`,
        `She took her own phone out to the landing, shut Ana's door behind her, and called Northglenn.`,
        ((s.flags.calledgenealiar||G.calledgenealiar)
          ?`<span class="said" data-who="Gene">"This is Vigil."</span> Business, from the first word, and a hose running somewhere behind him that he didn't bother to shut off for her. She asked about page eleven. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"Off the phone with the supervisor,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"Sunday night. Kitchen table."</span> A pause she could hear him decide about. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"I remember thinking, that's a lot of red."</span>`
          :`<span class="said" data-who="Gene">"It's Gene."</span> He was out on the drive with the hose; she could hear it running. She asked about page eleven. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"Off the phone with the supervisor,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"Sunday night, at the kitchen table. My wife was doing the crossword."</span> A pause. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"I remember thinking, that's a lot of red."</span>`),
        `Then she read him the November line off the yellow copy, word for word. The hose shut off. He let it sit. She could hear a sprinkler going two yards down from him.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Gene">"That's me,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"That line's mine. I called the lab and pulled it."</span> Another pause. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"I'll tell you why, and you won't like it. I got called to that bar ten, eleven times over the years, patrol and after. Every time one went out to the lot, it was the same two who'd started it inside. Every single time. So I figured the nails would tell me what I already knew, and the lab had a line out the door."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Eleven fights isn't very many to build a rule on, Gene. That's not even one a year."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Gene">"It's what I had,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"Eleven's more than none."</span> True. And a hundred would've had the same hole, if they'd all come to him the same way.`,
          why:`Flaw. Small, yes. But the deeper trouble is how the eleven got to him. A bigger pile of the same kind of calls would make the same mistake.`},
        {t:`"You pulled it because you knew what it would show. You didn't want to find another man."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Gene">"I've told you what I did,"</span> he says, flat, and she hears the hose start up again on his end. That's the end of the call, near enough. She'd gone after the man and left his reason standing.`,
          why:`Flaw. That's an accusation about motive, not a flaw in what he argued. His reasoning has a real hole, and this walks right past it.`},
        {t:`"Those were fights somebody called in, Gene. The ones nobody called in never got to you."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Nothing on the line. Then a sound like a laugh with no fun in it. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"Nobody calls about the ones that go fine,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"Or the ones that go out back where nobody's looking."</span> He's quiet again. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"Eleven I got called to. That's all I ever knew about that bar."</span>`,
          why:`Flaw. Gene builds a rule from the fights he was called to, and those aren't a fair sample of the bar's fights. A fight that went out back and ended where nobody saw it never became a call, so the rule was built from exactly the cases most likely to fit it.`},
        {t:`"Then you should've tested it anyway, Gene. If you were right, it would only have proved it."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Gene">"Should've,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"Sure. I've got a garage full of should've."</span> It's true, and it doesn't touch why he thought he was right.`,
          why:`Flaw. It's fair advice, but it doesn't name what's wrong with his reasoning. The question is why his rule didn't hold, and that's about where his cases came from.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s8`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR06`, tags:[`STAT`], title:`The city`, q:`What, if anything, is wrong with the caption's reasoning?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Sunday, May 16, she built the count at the kitchen table on her laptop, with page eleven copied out beside it and the 2007 box score open in another window. Game 3. A Saturday night at Coors Field, the Rockies' first World Series game at home, and Boston fans all over the city in red.`,
        `<prop type="chart" kind="bars" title="Red, the night of Saturday, October 27, 2007 (Dani's draft)" labels="At Coors Field for Game 3,On Gene's crew list,Crew besides Pa anybody interviewed,Men seen fighting Travis" values="49983,21,0,1">Under it, her caption: The city was full of red that night. Fifty thousand at the game alone, Boston everywhere. The jacket was the most common thing in Denver, so it couldn't point at anybody.</prop>`,
        `The first bar ran clear across the screen. The other three were slivers. She had her thumb on send a long while, and then she didn't send it. She read the caption the way Ana would, with a pencil she didn't have.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`The city's red doesn't count. Almost none of those fifty thousand were near that lot at 11:15.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`She deletes the first bar. Without it the chart looks foolish: twenty one, nothing, one. Three stubs. That's the chart. She sends Ana that one, with no caption at all, and Ana writes back a few minutes later. Better.`,
          why:`Flaw. The caption measures red across a whole city and draws a conclusion about one parking lot. The people to count are the ones who could have been at that curb at a quarter past eleven. Most of the city's red was miles away, so the city's number can't show the jacket meant nothing there.`},
        {t:`Fans in red jerseys aren't men in red jackets. Consuelo said jacket, not shirt, every time.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Meredith would make that point herself, first thing. Fix the word, though, and the long bar is still fifty thousand people miles away across town. She'd fixed a word inside the wrong number.`,
          why:`Flaw. A real distinction, and it would shrink the city's count. But the bigger problem is the place, not the garment. Even a count of red jackets in the whole city wouldn't be about that lot.`},
        {t:`It's right, but too small. It should count the whole metro area, every fan who watched in a bar.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Bigger. She sits looking at it and thinks of Ana's face. If fifty thousand people across town prove nothing, a million people across the metro just prove it louder.`,
          why:`Flaw. A larger city count makes the same mistake bigger. The relevant group is the people near the lot, not more people farther off.`},
        {t:`Nothing. If red was everywhere in Denver that night, then it was everywhere in that lot too.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`She types it and looks at it and can't make herself believe it. A refinery lot on Highway 2 isn't a sample of a city. It's one lot.`,
          why:`Flaw. It assumes the lot looked like the city. Red can be all over Denver and scarce in one lot on Highway 2, or the reverse, and the city's count won't tell you which.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s9`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR10`, tags:[`STAT`], title:`Who`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, May 18. She'd spent Sunday night on page eleven, and two lunch hours in her car with her own phone. A legal pad with twenty one lines and what she'd found beside each one. Four obituaries. One man in Texas. A union newsletter from 2011 with three of the names in a softball photo. Phone numbers for six.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Dani">"If I call all twenty,"</span> she said, <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"somebody remembers who went out back. Somebody saw something. We find the man at the curb."</span>`,
        ((s.flags.overclaimed||G.overclaimed)
          ?`Ana opened her folder and took out a photocopy of a page Dani had last seen face down on her own kitchen table in March. Her own sentence, with two pencil circles around proves. Ana had kept a copy. Of course she had. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"I said we'd talk about that word,"</span> she said, and laid it on the desk next to the legal pad.`
          :`Ana looked at the legal pad a long time, both hands flat on the desk on either side of it.`),
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"We don't have to prove who,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"We have to prove it wasn't him."</span>`,
        `At the trial the State had to show it was him, and in 2008 that had been Phil's whole closing. That was before a verdict. After one, the weight sits on your side of the table, and Ana had been saying so since March in one way or another.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"So tell me what your count shows,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"And what it doesn't."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"It shows one of the crew most likely did it. So we start calling and we find out which one."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Most likely,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Based on what? You've got a list of who was on a shift. You haven't got one of them in that lot."</span>`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Too strong. A list of men with the jacket doesn't put any of them at the curb, so it can't support that one of them probably did it.`},
        {t:`"It shows the jacket can't pick him out of twenty one. It doesn't put any of the others at that curb."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's the size of it,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Probably wasn't him, on the evidence, is what we owe the court. Who is somebody else's job. Maybe nobody's, ever."</span> She tears the legal pad page off and puts it in the folder. Not the trash. The folder.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Twenty one men had that jacket, so the jacket alone can't identify Luis. That's all the count supports. It doesn't put another crew member in the lot, and it doesn't settle where Luis was.`},
        {t:`"It shows twenty other men could just as easily have been the man at the curb that night."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Just as easily,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Could, yes. Just as easily, no. One of them had just been thrown out of the bar for fighting the man. Meredith will say so and she'll be right."</span>`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Close. Other men could have been there. But "just as easily" claims they were all equally likely, and the count doesn't show that, especially when one of them had just fought Travis.`},
        {t:`"It shows Pa almost certainly wasn't the man at the curb. One in twenty one is under five percent."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"On the jacket alone,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Nobody's case is the jacket alone. Not hers and not ours. Say almost certainly in Brighton and she'll stand up and thank you."</span>`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Too strong. One in twenty one is what the jacket alone is worth. Other evidence points at Luis, so the count can't make him almost certainly innocent.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s10`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR07`, tags:[`STAT`], title:`After`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"If you call anybody,"</span> Ana said, <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"you call to ask one thing, and you ask it nicely. Meredith says the jacket wasn't common. We say it was common enough that it couldn't point at one man in that lot. What fact, if we could get it, does the most for us?"</span>`,
        `Downstairs a washer hit its spin cycle and the whole floor hummed with it. Dicta opened one eye and shut it.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`That every man on the crew list was issued the very same red jacket by the refinery that year.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Issued,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The state issued me a pager in 1996. I couldn't tell you where it was on any night that year."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen. It helps a little, since it means twenty one jackets existed. But a jacket in a closet in Thornton can't be the jacket at the curb. The claim needs jackets that were there.`},
        {t:`That Boston fans had bought red jackets all over Denver that weekend, at the game and after.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's your Sunday chart again,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Fifty thousand people who weren't there."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen. Red across the city isn't red near the lot. It's the same mistake as the city bar on the chart.`},
        {t:`That a dozen of the crew were still in the bar in their jackets after Ronnie threw Pa out.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"After,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's the word."</span> She writes it on a card, STILL THERE AFTER 10:45?, and puts it on top of page eleven. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Ask that. Don't ask them who. Nobody's going to tell a stranger on the phone who, and anyway it isn't our question."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen. The claim is that the jacket couldn't single Luis out in the lot. The jackets that matter are the ones still around after he left, because those are the men who could have been out there at a quarter past eleven. A count of them supports the claim most directly.`},
        {t:`That Pa had worn that same jacket into the Night Owl every Saturday for years before that one.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's Meredith's,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It makes him the regular in red. You just argued her habit evidence for her."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen. Reversed. It ties Luis more tightly to the jacket, which helps the State, not the claim.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s11`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR08`, tags:[`STAT`], title:`Twenty`, q:`What should Dani make of what Shannon said?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday night a Denver news site ran it. Celia didn't send it this time. Dani found it herself at eleven, in bed, which was the wrong place.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="News item, Tuesday, May 18, 2027">Prosecutors in Adams County won't oppose DNA testing in the case of a man convicted of a 2007 death outside a Commerce City bar, according to a court filing. Luis Ruiz, 62, is serving forty years for second degree murder in the death of Travis Hensley, 31. His lawyer says evidence collected in 2007 was never tested, and that the red work jacket at the center of the trial was common among men in the bar that night. Shannon Hensley, the victim's sister, said she wasn't surprised. "Let them count jackets," she said. "Say there were twenty. One of them fought my brother and went out the back after him."</prop>`,
        `On the nightstand, in her own capitals from Sunday, before Ana: SAY 20 JACKETS IN THE BAR. PA IS ONE. 19 IN 20 IT WASN'T HIM.`,
        `She read Shannon's sentence four times. Then her own card. Then the sentence again.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Lots of the Boston fans wore red jerseys too. Twenty jackets leaves out every red shirt in the room.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`That only makes her number bigger. She'd gone looking for what was wrong with her card and come back with more of it.`,
          why:`Weaken. Adding red shirts pushes the base rate further in Dani's favor, so it can't weaken her argument. And a jersey isn't the jacket Consuelo saw.`},
        {t:`She's wrong, and somebody ought to tell her. Write Shannon tonight and walk her through the twenty.`, s:0, flag:`wroteshannon`, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`She writes it at the kitchen table at one in the morning, two pages, the count and the percent and the cap, and finds an address for a Shannon Hensley in Westminster on her own laptop in less time than it takes the kettle to boil, which is its own kind of awful. She mails it on the way to work. She doesn't tell Ana.`,
          why:`Weaken. Not an answer. It doesn't touch the argument on the card, and Shannon's sentence is the best weakener in the room. Arguing with a dead man's sister by mail doesn't answer it. It just makes her read the count again.`},
        {t:`The twenty weren't alike. Only one of them had just fought Travis and been thrown out the back.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`She doesn't write to Shannon. She writes Shannon's sentence on the card under her own, in Shannon's words, because it's the better argument and somebody's going to have to answer it. Then she turns the light off and lies there a while, thinking about a woman who'd had to say that to a reporter on the phone.`,
          why:`Weaken. A base rate treats every man in a group as equally likely. The twenty jackets weren't equal: one belonged to the man who had just fought Travis and gone out the back. A fact that sets one member apart cuts the base rate's force.`},
        {t:`Nobody knows how many of the crew were really in the bar. Twenty is a guess, and maybe a high one.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It's true. Ana would say so. But make it ten jackets and it's still one in ten, and Shannon's sentence would still be sitting there on her phone, untouched.`,
          why:`Weaken. Fair enough, twenty is a guess. Cut it in half and Luis is still one in ten. What really hurts the card is that one of those jackets wasn't like the others, and this choice never gets near that.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s12`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR17`, title:`Queen`, q:`Which question would best test the State's timeline?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday, May 19, seven. Her step. The recording, and then him, before she could say anything at all. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Queen goes home."</span>`,
        `She'd carried the board down to the step for once, and she moved it there on her knees. His queen off the long file, back to the square it started the game on, and the file in front of her king was just a file again, closed, with nobody at the far end of it. Four weeks he'd looked at it. That was what he'd been looking at.`,
        ((s.flags.pushedluis||G.pushedluis)
          ?`He didn't say anything else about it. Since Sterling the board had gotten the same five words from him every week, and now it had gotten its move.`
          :`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"You saw it,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"At the table. I watched you see it."</span>`),
        `He wanted the news, so she gave it to him. Meredith wasn't fighting the test. Ana said the judge could sign any day, and then the lab would get the scrapings, and nobody could say how long after that. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"And then they know,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Whatever it is."</span>`,
        ((s.flags.promisedhim||G.promisedhim)
          ?`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"And then you get me out,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"That's how it goes, right?"</span> He laughed after, so she'd know it was a joke. She didn't laugh, and he heard her not laugh.`
          :`He didn't ask her what she hoped it was. He never asked her that.`),
        `Ana's card was on the step beside her, the State's timeline in pencil.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Ana's timeline card">10:45, OUT THE BACK.<br>11:04, STILL OUT HERE.<br>11:15, MAN IN RED AT THE CURB, RAN NORTH ON FOOT.<br>11:21, POLICE.</prop>`,
        `Thirty minutes from the back door to the curb, and the State needed him in that lot the whole time, or gone and back. She had him on the line, and maybe one question before the recording told them a minute was left.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Pa, why'd you go to the Night Owl that night at all, with Ma working and nobody home?"`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Saturday,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"The Series. Where else was I going to watch it?"</span> A fine answer. It tells her nothing about a quarter past eleven.`,
          why:`Evaluate the argument. Why he went in has nothing to do with whether he was at the curb at 11:15. Either answer leaves the timeline where it was.`},
        {t:`"Pa, did anybody see you leave the Night Owl that night? Anybody at all, out in the lot?"`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Nobody I know of,"</span> he says. And nobody was asked, so nobody would know. A yes would have meant something. A no means what the patrol report already said.`,
          why:`Evaluate the argument. A yes would help, but a no tells you nothing new, because nobody in that lot was ever asked. An evaluate answer has to matter whichever way it comes back.`},
        {t:`"Pa, what did Travis say to you inside, right before you hit him? What was it?"`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`He doesn't answer for so long she can hear the tier behind him, a TV, somebody laughing at it. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Nothing worth saying again,"</span> he says. Wrong question for the clock, and he'd known that before she did.`,
          why:`Evaluate the argument. That's about the fight inside at 10:25. The State's 11:15 timeline doesn't turn on what was said in the bar.`},
        {t:`"Pa, after Ronnie threw you out, was your truck still in that lot at a quarter past eleven?"`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`It's quiet a long time. Somebody on his end is yelling about the microwave. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I drove home, mija,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I told you. I told everybody."</span> It's an answer. It isn't to the question she asked, which was about a quarter past eleven. She lets it sit there on the line between them and doesn't take it back.`,
          why:`Evaluate the argument. The State needs him in that lot from 10:45 to 11:15, or gone and back. Say the truck was still there at 11:15: he never left, which fits the State's timeline, though it has to explain why he ran off on foot. Say it was gone: he'd driven away, and the State has to explain how he got back to the curb without it. One answer helps the timeline and the other hurts it, and that's the test.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills: [
    { after:`f8s1`, title:`Share and count`, setup:[`Meredith's response says about 120 people were in the Night Owl that night and the crew list has 21 names, so fewer than one in five people in that room wore the jacket. Ana wants the numbers worked out on paper both ways, the share and the count.`,`Share = count / total, as a percent. Round to two decimals.`],
      fields:[{label:`Crew share of the room, percent`, answer:17.5, tol:0.01},{label:`Jackets in the room besides Luis's, if all 21 came`, answer:20, tol:0},{label:`Crew share if the room had held 240 people, percent`, answer:8.75, tol:0.01},{label:`Jackets besides Luis's in that bigger room`, answer:20, tol:0}],
      solution:[`21 / 120 = 0.175, or 17.50 percent. Meredith's right that it's under one in five.`,`21 jackets with one of them Luis's leaves 20 others.`,`Double the room and the share halves: 21 / 240 = 0.0875, or 8.75 percent.`,`The count doesn't move at all. Still 20 other jackets. A share tells you how the jacket compares with the room. An identification has to know how many men the jacket fits, and the answer has to be one. A smaller percent makes twenty men sound like fewer. It doesn't make them fewer.`] },
    { after:`f8s10`, title:`Out of how many`, setup:[`Nobody knows how many of the 21 men on page eleven were in the Night Owl that night, so Ana made Dani write down a high guess and a low guess before anybody called anybody. Luis counts as one of the crew both times.`,`High guess: 15 of the crew in the bar, plus 5 Boston fans in red jackets. Low guess: 8 of the crew, plus 2 fans. On the jacket alone, Luis's share = 1 / red jackets in the room, as a percent.`],
      fields:[{label:`Red jackets in the room, high guess`, answer:20, tol:0},{label:`Luis's share on the high guess, percent`, answer:5, tol:0.01},{label:`Red jackets in the room, low guess`, answer:10, tol:0},{label:`Luis's share on the low guess, percent`, answer:10, tol:0.01}],
      solution:[`High: 15 + 5 = 20 jackets. Luis is 1 of 20, so 1 / 20 = 0.05, or 5 percent.`,`Low: 8 + 2 = 10 jackets. 1 / 10 = 0.10, or 10 percent.`,`Halve the guess and his share doubles, and it's still one in ten. That's what a base rate is for. On the jacket alone, the jacket never came close to picking him out.`,`What it can't do is the rest. A base rate treats every jacket in the room the same, and these weren't the same. One of them had just been in a fight with Travis. The number tells you what the jacket was worth. It doesn't tell you who was at the curb. Ana wrote that under the numbers, smaller.`] }
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f8s8`, title:`What does this number actually show`, intro:`Ana's index cards, a stack held together with a binder clip. Each one has a number somebody used to prove something. Pick what the number actually shows.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Card one. A sign in the diner window: "Commerce City's favorite pie, voted by 92 percent!" The ballot box sat by the register.`, choices:[`Most people in Commerce City like this pie best.`,`Most of the votes cast at the register picked this pie.`,`Only 8 percent of the city dislikes the pie.`], answer:1, why:`The voters were already eating there. The sign counted its own customers and called them a city.`},
      {prompt:`Card two. "Burglaries on our block doubled this year." Last year there were two.`, choices:[`There were four this year.`,`Burglary is a growing crisis on the block.`,`Next year there will probably be eight.`], answer:0, why:`Doubled from two is four. That's all it shows. A doubling sounds like a wave until somebody asks for the count.`},
      {prompt:`Card three. "Seventy percent of the accidents at the plant last year happened on the day shift."`, choices:[`The day shift is the more careless shift.`,`The night shift is the safer one to work.`,`Where the accidents fell, not which shift is riskier.`], answer:2, why:`If seventy percent of the work happens on days, seventy percent of the accidents is just the work. You need the share of the hours before the share of the accidents means anything.`},
      {prompt:`Card four. The school's average reading score rose from 71 to 74 after the new program started. Over the summer, twelve of its lowest scoring students moved away.`, choices:[`The new program raised the scores.`,`The average rose, maybe without the program.`,`The program hurt the students who left.`], answer:1, why:`Take twelve low scores out and the average goes up all by itself. The program might have helped. This number can't say.`},
      {prompt:`Card five. "One in three jackets in the lost and found is red."`, choices:[`Of the jackets in that bin, a third are red.`,`People lose red jackets more than other colors.`,`Red is the most common jacket color in town.`], answer:0, why:`That's the bin, and only the bin. Ask how many jackets are in it. If it's three, it's one red jacket.`},
      {prompt:`Card six. "Nine out of ten dentists surveyed recommend Glintwell." Glintwell paid for the survey and chose the dentists.`, choices:[`Most dentists recommend Glintwell.`,`One dentist in ten dislikes Glintwell.`,`Nine in ten of the dentists Glintwell picked said so.`], answer:2, why:`Whoever picks the dentists picks the answer, near enough. That's a survey of Glintwell's friends.`}
    ]},
  passages: {
    p1: { title:`The People's response to the defendant's motion, filed Monday, May 3, 2027, excerpt`,
      text:[`The People do not object to DNA testing of the fingernail scrapings, and so stipulate in writing. The conditions the statute sets for testing are met, and the People will not pretend otherwise. The People object to everything else in the motion.`,
        `The People expect the scrapings to show the defendant's DNA. He fought the victim inside the bar less than an hour before the assault, as Mr. Galvan testified. A result showing the defendant's DNA would confirm what the jury heard. If the results show anything else, the People will address it then.`,
        `The rest of the motion asks the Court to retry a case on arguments the defense could have made in 2008. A ball cap in a dispatch log the defense had before trial is not new. A child heard crying near a bar is not evidence of who struck Travis Hensley. And the red jacket was not common. Of about one hundred twenty people in the bar, the crew list in Detective Vigil's notes has twenty one names. Fewer than one in five people in that room wore the jacket, and exactly one man in it was seen fighting the victim.`] },
    p2: { title:`Dani's practice set: counting houses in 1854`,
      text:[`In July 1854 cholera came back to London, and with it an old argument about where the disease came from. The common view held that it rose from foul air. The physician John Snow had argued since 1849 that it traveled in water fouled by the waste of the sick. A count of the dead could not settle it. Crowded districts lost more people than spacious ones, but crowded districts had worse air and worse water at the same time, so a count of deaths only showed where people had died.`,
        `What Snow needed was two groups alike in nearly every way except their water, and south London happened to have them. Two private companies, the Southwark and Vauxhall Company and the Lambeth Company, piped water down the same streets, often to houses side by side, and many customers could not have said which company served them. In 1852 the Lambeth Company had moved its intake up the Thames to Thames Ditton, above the reach of the city's sewage. Southwark and Vauxhall still drew from the river at Battersea, below it.`,
        `Snow and a helper went to the houses where people had died and asked which company supplied the water. When a household didn't know, he tested a sample, since the Southwark and Vauxhall water carried far more salt than Lambeth's, which a simple chemical test could show. In the first seven weeks of the epidemic, he found, 1,263 people died in houses supplied by Southwark and Vauxhall and 98 in houses supplied by Lambeth.`,
        `Those two figures alone prove less than they seem to. Southwark and Vauxhall served more houses, 40,046 against Lambeth's 26,107, by a return made to Parliament, so it would have had more deaths even if its water were no worse. Snow's next step was to divide. For every 10,000 houses, Southwark and Vauxhall's had 315 deaths, Lambeth's had 37, and the rest of London, with its mix of water and districts, had 59. With the size of the two companies taken out, the difference was still between eight and nine times.`,
        `Textbooks now call the study an early natural experiment, and they are right to. The method behind it is plainer than the name. Snow went past the large number to the question of what it was out of, and he picked two groups that differed in little besides the water from their taps. His rate does not by itself prove what causes cholera. It sets a fact in front of any explanation, one the explanation has to account for, and an eight or nine to one difference between neighbors on the same street leaves the foul air theory very little room.`] }
  },
  quiz: [
    {q:`Transit official: Last year, cyclists were involved in 9 percent of the traffic accidents reported in Maren County. This year, after the county painted new bike lanes on its busiest roads, cyclists were involved in only 6 percent of reported accidents. Clearly, fewer cyclists are getting into accidents since the lanes went in.<br><br>The reasoning in the official's argument is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it`,
      opts:[`assumes that the new bike lanes were painted where most cyclist accidents had happened`,`fails to consider that some accidents involving cyclists are never reported at all`,`treats the opinion of a transit official as if it settled a question of fact`,`takes a drop in cyclists' share of accidents for a drop in their number of accidents`,`overlooks the possibility that drivers have become more careful near the new lanes`],
      a:3, why:`Flaw. A share can fall while the count rises. If total accidents grew enough, 6 percent of this year's accidents could be more cyclists than 9 percent of last year's. Where the lanes went, unreported accidents and careful drivers don't touch that move, and nothing rests on the official's authority.`, task:`LR06`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`STAT`]},
    {q:`Station manager: During Tuesday's morning show we invited listeners to call in and say whether the city should build a new downtown stadium. Of the 412 people who called, 301 opposed the stadium. So most of the city's voters will vote against the stadium measure in November.<br><br>Which one of the following most accurately describes a flaw in the manager's reasoning?`,
      opts:[`It relies on a sample of 412 callers, which is far too few people to say anything about any group.`,`It draws a conclusion about all voters from callers who chose to respond and may not be typical.`,`It assumes that people who oppose the stadium now won't change their minds before November.`,`It takes for granted that the people who called in all listen to the station's morning show.`,`It confuses opposing a new stadium with opposing any public spending on sports at all.`],
      a:1, why:`Flaw. People who bother to call a radio show picked themselves, they're likely to feel strongly, and they may not look like the city's voters. Four hundred people chosen at random could say a lot, so size isn't the core problem. Minds can change, but that's secondary. The callers were listeners by definition, and nothing mixes up two kinds of opposition.`, task:`LR06`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`STAT`]},
    {q:`Insurance agent: More than half of all car accidents happen within five miles of the driver's home. So driving near home is more dangerous than driving on long trips, and drivers should take special care on short errands around the neighborhood.<br><br>The agent's argument is flawed because it`,
      opts:[`assumes that drivers who take long trips never drive near their own homes`,`treats a claim about most accidents as if it were a claim about all of them`,`relies on accident figures that might all be drawn from a single year`,`presumes that special care could prevent every accident on short errands`,`fails to consider how much of all driving happens within five miles of home`],
      a:4, why:`Flaw. If most driving happens close to home, most accidents will too, even if a mile driven there is no riskier than any other. The figure means nothing without the share of driving done near home. The other choices name mistakes the agent never makes.`, task:`LR06`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`STAT`]},
    {q:`Library director: Over the past year, more bicycles were stolen from the rack by our front door than from any other bicycle rack downtown. That rack is clearly the least secure in the city, and we should move it around to the side of the building.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most seriously weakens the director's argument?`,
      opts:[`Reported thefts from bicycle racks downtown rose slightly last year over the year before.`,`The library has kept a bicycle rack beside its front door for more than twenty years.`,`The library's front rack holds far more bicycles on a typical day than any other rack downtown.`,`Bicycle thieves downtown most often use bolt cutters, which work on any common lock.`,`Some bicycle owners don't report a theft to the police when the stolen bike was cheap.`],
      a:2, why:`Weaken. More thefts can come from more bikes. If the front rack holds far more bikes than any other, it can top the list while each bike there is no likelier to be taken. A citywide rise, the rack's age, thieves' tools and unreported cheap bikes don't touch that comparison.`, task:`LR08`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`STAT`]},
    {q:`Real estate columnist: The average price of a home sold in Fallow Creek last spring was 40 percent higher than the average the spring before. Ordinary houses in Fallow Creek have clearly become far more expensive, and young families are being priced out of the town.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most seriously weakens the columnist's argument?`,
      opts:[`Last spring's sales included four new lakefront houses, the priciest the town has ever seen.`,`Interest rates on home loans were about the same in both of the springs.`,`The columnist has written about housing in Fallow Creek for a good many years.`,`Homes in the towns all around Fallow Creek also rose in price last spring.`,`Somewhat fewer homes in Fallow Creek changed hands last spring than in the spring before that.`],
      a:0, why:`Weaken. An average can jump because of a few unusual sales. If four record lakefront houses were in last spring's mix, the average can rise sharply while ordinary houses cost what they did. Fewer sales alone doesn't say which houses sold, and the rest either support the claim or don't touch it.`, task:`LR08`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`STAT`]},
    {q:`School board member: Last fall the district moved the start time at Ridley Middle School from 7:45 to 9:00, while Carver Middle School kept the earlier time. Over the school year, Ridley's students were absent 20 percent less often than Carver's. The later start time reduced absences.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the board member's argument?`,
      opts:[`Students at Ridley told a district survey that they liked the new start time.`,`The district plans to move Carver's start time to 9:00 starting next fall.`,`Absences at both of the schools rose during the winter flu season.`,`The two schools had nearly the same absence rates for years before the change.`,`Ridley also added a free breakfast program for its students the same fall.`],
      a:3, why:`Strengthen. If the schools' absence rates matched before the change, the gap that opened afterward is harder to put down to some old difference between them. Liking the time, the district's plans and the flu season don't bear on the cause, and the breakfast program is a rival explanation.`, task:`LR07`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`STAT`,`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`In the Tamsin Valley orchard district, the number of acres planted in apples grew by 10 percent between 2015 and 2025. Over the same period, apples' share of the district's total planted acreage fell from 60 percent to 50 percent.<br><br>If the statements above are true, which one of the following is most strongly supported?`,
      opts:[`Fewer acres were planted in apples in 2025 than in 2015.`,`The district's total planted acreage grew between 2015 and 2025.`,`Apple growers in the district earned less in 2025 than in 2015.`,`Some acres of apples were replanted with other fruit by 2025.`,`Acres planted in pears grew faster than acres planted in apples.`],
      a:1, why:`Most strongly supported. Apple acres went up while their share went down, so everything else must have grown faster, and the total with it. Fewer apple acres in 2025 contradicts the passage. Earnings and replanting aren't addressed, and pears are never mentioned.`, task:`LR10`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`STAT`]},
    {q:`Biologist: In the Dunlin marsh, red-winged blackbirds nest only in stands of cattails at least a meter tall. Last spring, after a dry winter, nearly every cattail in the marsh was under half a meter high when nesting season began. Even so, volunteers counted the usual number of blackbird pairs feeding in the marsh all spring.<br><br>The biologist's statements, if true, most strongly support which one of the following?`,
      opts:[`Red-winged blackbirds prefer to feed in marshes where the cattails are short.`,`The region's blackbird population grew last spring despite the dry winter.`,`Next spring the cattails in the Dunlin marsh will grow back past a meter.`,`The volunteers miscounted the blackbird pairs in the marsh last spring.`,`Most pairs feeding in the Dunlin marsh last spring didn't start out nesting there.`],
      a:4, why:`Most strongly supported. These birds nest only in cattails a meter tall, and almost none were that tall when the season began, so most of the usual pairs feeding there can't have been nesting in the marsh then. Nothing shows a preference for short cattails, a regional rise, what happens next spring, or a miscount.`, task:`LR10`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Shop owner: For years the candy rack in my shop stood in the back corner, past the bread. In March I moved it up next to the register, where every customer waits in line. Since then, candy sales have risen by a quarter. Moving the rack is what did it.<br><br>Which one of the following would be most useful to know in evaluating the shop owner's argument?`,
      opts:[`whether the shop's customers generally prefer chocolate to other kinds of candy`,`how much the shop owner paid for the new candy rack in March`,`whether sales of everything else in the shop also rose by about a quarter`,`whether other shops in the area carry the same brands of candy`,`how many different kinds of candy the shop carried before March`],
      a:2, why:`Evaluate the argument. If everything in the shop rose by a quarter, candy just rode a general rise and the rack gets no credit. If only candy rose, the rack looks like the cause. The answer matters either way. None of the others would change how strong the argument is.`, task:`LR17`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`STAT`,`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`Every volunteer at the Elk Ridge food bank who works a Saturday shift is trained to drive the delivery van. No one who is trained to drive the van is under twenty one. Talia volunteers at the Elk Ridge food bank, and she is nineteen.<br><br>If the statements above are true, which one of the following must also be true?`,
      opts:[`Talia doesn't work a Saturday shift at the food bank.`,`Talia works only weekday shifts at the food bank.`,`Most food bank volunteers are twenty one or older.`,`Talia will be trained to drive the van at twenty one.`,`Everyone trained to drive the van works Saturdays.`],
      a:0, why:`Must be true. Saturday volunteers are all trained, and trained people are all at least twenty one. Talia is nineteen, so she isn't trained, so she doesn't work Saturdays. She could work Sundays, so "only weekday" goes too far. Nothing says most, nothing promises training later, and the rule doesn't run backward.`, task:`LR09`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`Editor: Our paper runs a story that makes a claim about someone's private conduct only if the claim is confirmed by two independent sources. Pruneda's draft about the county assessor rests on a single source. So the draft can't run as it's written.<br><br>The editor's conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?`,
      opts:[`A single source is usually less reliable than two independent sources would be.`,`The county assessor would object to the story if it ran as written.`,`Every story the paper runs has to have at least one named source.`,`Pruneda has relied on single sources in a number of earlier drafts.`,`Pruneda's draft makes a claim about the assessor's private conduct.`],
      a:4, why:`Sufficient assumption. The rule only applies to claims about private conduct. Add that this draft makes one, and with a single source it can't run. Reliability in general, the assessor's reaction, a one source minimum and Pruneda's habits don't close the gap.`, task:`LR05`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`At Ferrand General Hospital, patients operated on by Dr. Abara had a higher death rate in the month after surgery than the patients of any other surgeon on staff. Yet the hospital's review board, which examined every one of those operations, rated Dr. Abara the most skillful surgeon on the staff.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy described above?`,
      opts:[`Dr. Abara has worked at Ferrand General longer than most of its other surgeons.`,`Dr. Abara is the surgeon the hospital sends its most dangerous and complicated cases to.`,`The review board meets only twice a year to examine the hospital's operations.`,`Death rates after surgery at Ferrand General are lower than the region's average.`,`Dr. Abara performs fewer operations each year than most of the other surgeons.`],
      a:1, why:`Resolve the paradox. If the hardest cases go to Dr. Abara, a higher death rate is just what a skilled surgeon taking the worst risks would show. Tenure, the board's schedule, regional rates and caseload don't explain both facts at once.`, task:`LR18`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`STAT`]},
    {q:`According to the response, the People expect the scrapings to show the defendant's DNA because`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`he fought the victim inside the bar before the assault`,`he wore the same red jacket as the man at the curb`,`he was the only man in the bar seen near the victim`,`his DNA was found on the victim's shirt back in 2007`,`the defense has agreed that the testing is truly needed`],
      a:0, why:`Specific detail. The response says the People expect his DNA because he fought the victim inside the bar less than an hour before the assault. It never ties the expectation to the jacket, says only one man was seen near the victim, mentions the shirt, or says the defense agreed to anything.`, task:`RC05`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`It can be inferred from the response that if the test finds DNA from a man other than the defendant, the People`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`will agree that the defendant should have a new trial`,`will ask the court to stop the testing before it's done`,`haven't yet committed to what they'll argue about it`,`will say that the lab must have made a mistake`,`will drop the case against the defendant entirely`],
      a:2, why:`Inference. "If the results show anything else, the People will address it then" commits them to answering later without saying how. A new trial, stopping the test, blaming the lab and dropping the case all claim an answer the response deliberately doesn't give.`, task:`RC06`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main point of the passage?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`Snow proved in 1854 that cholera was caused by a germ carried in the river water of London.`,`The Lambeth Company's move upriver in 1852 saved more lives than any other change of the period.`,`Counting deaths house by house was the first reliable way of tracing where a disease began.`,`Snow made his case by comparing death rates, not counts, in groups that differed in little but their water.`,`Snow's critics were wrong to believe that cholera rose from the foul air of crowded districts.`],
      a:3, why:`Main idea. The passage explains why a count of deaths couldn't settle the question, how two companies on the same streets made a fair comparison, and how dividing by houses turned counts into rates. "Proved" and "germ" go past the passage, which says the rate isn't by itself a proof. Lambeth's move and the critics are parts of the story, and nothing calls house counts the first reliable method.`, task:`RC01`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`The author's attitude toward Snow's study is best described as`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`doubtful that the comparison was as fair as Snow himself believed it was`,`impatient with the praise it gets, which the author thinks it doesn't deserve`,`indifferent, treating it as a curiosity of nineteenth century medicine`,`hostile toward its method of going door to door to the houses of the dead`,`admiring, while careful about how much the rate by itself establishes`],
      a:4, why:`Author's attitude. The author says the textbooks are right about the study and that an eight or nine to one difference leaves the foul air theory very little room, but adds that the rate doesn't by itself prove the cause. Nothing doubts the fairness of the comparison, calls the praise undeserved, treats the study as a curiosity, or objects to the house visits.`, task:`RC03`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`The author would be most likely to agree with which one of the following statements?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`A large number of deaths in one group is enough to show what caused them.`,`A count of deaths says little until it's set against what it's drawn from.`,`The rest of London had the safest water of any group in Snow's study.`,`Natural experiments are less reliable than studies a scientist designs.`,`Households that didn't know their water supplier were best left out.`],
      a:1, why:`Would agree. The passage says Snow went past the large number to what it was out of, and it shows the raw counts proving less than they seem. The rest of London had more deaths per house than Lambeth's customers, nothing ranks natural experiments, and Snow tested the water of households that didn't know.`, task:`RC04`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which one of the following, if true, would most weaken the claim that Snow's two groups were alike in nearly every way except their water?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`Southwark and Vauxhall mostly served the poorest, most crowded houses on the shared streets.`,`Some households told Snow the wrong company when he asked who supplied their water.`,`Cholera returned to London in later years after the two companies had both improved their water.`,`The Lambeth Company charged its customers somewhat more than Southwark and Vauxhall did for water.`,`Snow's figures for the number of houses each company served came from a return made to Parliament.`],
      a:0, why:`Strengthen or weaken a passage claim. If one company's customers were the poorest and most crowded, the groups differed in more than water, and crowding could explain part of the gap. A few wrong answers from households would blur the count without making the groups differ in some other way, later years don't bear on 1854, a small price difference says little about the people, and the source of the house counts is already in the passage.`, task:`RC13`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AN`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `Thursday, May 20, at 2:14, her phone buzzed on her desk at the office with Ana's name on it, and she took it out to the stairwell, the way she took every call that wasn't the office's.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Signed,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"I'm sending you the page. Read it on the stairs."</span>`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="District Court, Adams County. Order on the defendant's motion for postconviction DNA testing, Thursday, May 20, 2027">On the People's written stipulation, the Court finds that the requirements for testing are met. The fingernail scrapings collected from Travis Hensley on October 28, 2007, Commerce City Police Department Item 7, shall be released to the Colorado Bureau of Investigation for DNA testing. The parties shall confer on the methods of testing. The defendant shall provide a reference sample. The Court reserves ruling on the remaining claims until the results are filed. Martin Esquibel, District Court Judge.</prop>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Dani">"When?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"When they get to it. They have a line, and now we're in it."</span> A bus sighed at the stop on Federal behind her. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"And whatever's under there goes into the state's databases and the national one. That's the law too. It runs both directions."</span> Then: <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Go back to work. Write your father tonight. He'll want it on paper."</span>`,
    `She wrote him at the kitchen table after supper, one page, the judge's sentence copied out word for word, because he'd read it six times and he'd want the words. Then she couldn't study. She sat with the prep book open to a page she'd already done and did the only thing she'd done all month. She counted.`,
    `Twenty one names on page eleven. A hundred twenty people in the room, if Ronnie was right. Eleven fights Gene got called to. Forty nine thousand nine hundred eighty three at Coors Field. One man seen fighting, by the one man anybody asked. Everybody in that bar had finally been counted by somebody, years late. Everybody but one.`,
    `A beer and a Sprite, every time he came up. Later on, just the Sprite.`,
    `Ronnie never counted the Sprite. Nobody had. A Sprite isn't anybody. It's a glass going back to a table by the hallway, where a big man in a wet shirt was bent over some, yelling down at something.`,
    `Teo drank Sprite. At ten he'd have had it with every meal if anybody let him. Their mother bought it in twelve packs and hid one in the dryer so he couldn't finish it in a weekend, and he always found it, and she always acted surprised.`,
    `Ten years old, Pa had said at Sterling, about the pawn. Some kid. Nobody. A boy crying Papi in the lot at ten to eleven. Teo had called him Papi until he was eleven or so, when he was hurt or scared, and Pa the rest of the time. She'd never forgotten it. In Consuelo's kitchen in February she'd thought of him for one second, and put the thought back where it came from, and kept it there.`,
    `The envelope was in the book, marking her page. She took it out and turned it over. WHO WAS THE KID? in her capitals from the Sterling lot. Under it she wrote his name. Not in capitals.`,
    `Teo`,
    `There were six numbers from page eleven in her phone, and she hadn't called one of them. She didn't call them now.`,
    `She looked at his name. Then at her phone. He hadn't answered the last one, the pawn, in almost five weeks.`,
    ...((s.flags.wroteshannon||G.wroteshannon)
      ?[`The letter to Westminster had been in the mail since Wednesday morning, in her handwriting, with her name at the bottom. She thought about that too. It didn't come out to anything either.`]
      :[]),
    `Three weeks from Saturday she'd sit in a test center for most of a morning with her phone in a locker. Seats opened next Tuesday, and she'd ask for Saturday the twelfth, the one morning nobody had to take off work for. She could drive herself. She'd driven herself to Sterling in the dark.`,
    ((s.flags.pushedteo||G.pushedteo)
      ?`After the party she'd have bet on a day before he answered anything, or on nothing at all. She typed it anyway, the way their mother had typed every text she ever sent, a capital at the front and a period at the end, like a letter.`
      :`She typed it the way their mother had typed every text she ever sent, a capital at the front and a period at the end, like a letter.`),
    `<prop type="text" from="Dani" time="9:41 PM">Can you drive me on the 12th.</prop>`,
    `She put the phone face down on the envelope and got as far as standing up. It buzzed before she'd pushed her chair in. Four seconds, maybe.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Teo" time="9:41 PM">yeah.</prop>`,
    `She sat back down. Downstairs the new washer had finished its load an hour ago, and she left it there. She left the envelope where it was, his name up, and turned the phone over so she could see that word too. Then she opened the book to a page she hadn't done yet. Page 212. A dog groomer in Fort Collins, a coupon, a bad inference about Tuesdays. She got it wrong. She did it again.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch08.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`Test Day`
});
